Follow the shadow setting for the popups of the browser

QtCurve gives a drop shadow to a menu, a tooltip and a combo box list,
and the popupShadow key switches that shadow off. The chrome of the
browser paints a shadow of its own and reads no key of QtCurve. A user
who switched the shadow off therefore kept it inside Firefox.

The sheet now carries one rule of the menus and the panels, which
popup.css of omni.ja reads for the content of each one. A true key
takes the figures of the shadow that QtCurve draws for a popup of Qt.
qtcX11ShadowInit() mixes a grey of 102 beside the window, and its
gradient falls to zero across the shadowSize key. A probe fits the
Gaussian of the browser to that curve. The blur is 0.220 of the key
and the spread is 0.088 of it, at a root mean square error of 0.036.

The room row holds the blur and the spread together, because the
browser clips the shadow at that margin. The row carries no
!important, because popup.css holds the same property at 0px under
that weight where the window manager gives no alpha. The GTK sheet of
QtCurve holds a fixed token, which reads no key of the size.

A false key holds the property at none and states no room. An absent
key gives the default of true, so an older settings file needs no
migration. A tooltip takes the native appearance of the platform, and
this property does not reach it. The static theme carries no rule of
this kind, because a manifest of the browser holds colours alone.

assert-ff-shadow.sh writes four passes over a private home at two
sizes. It reads the pixel figures, the grey, the room and the weight
of each pass, and 14 doctored copies must fail it.

The change sits on the branch work/firefox-theme of the local tree, as
commit 7 of 14. The branch holds those fourteen commits above the head of
work/gtk4-theme, which merge request !15 on system/qtcurve holds. The fork
wyn/qtcurve holds work/firefox-theme. Merge request !29 on system/qtcurve
holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

